            <fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/Y4Y8/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>Y4Y8</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/Y4Y8/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Gating splits the up-projection in two">Gating splits the up-projection in two</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="anki">1787063265996</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>For one <fr:link href="/YB7M/" title="Transformer" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/YB7M/" display-uri="YB7M" type="local">Transformer</fr:link> layer with a gated MLP, what are the parameter count and training FLOPs — and why is the coefficient three rather than two?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  <html:strong>Parameters:</html:strong> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[3DF]]></fr:tex>, from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[W_{in1}[D,F]]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[W_{in2}[D,F]]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[W_{out}[F,D]]]></fr:tex>.

  <html:strong>Training FLOPs:</html:strong> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[18BTDF]]></fr:tex> — three matmuls at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[6BTDF]]></fr:tex> each, by the <fr:link href="/92HA/" title="The backward pass is two matmuls, not one" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/92HA/" display-uri="92HA" type="local"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[6NPM]]></fr:tex></fr:link> rule.

  <html:strong>Where the third matrix comes from.</html:strong> A <fr:link href="/K2N7/" title="Gated Linear Unit (GLU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/K2N7/" display-uri="K2N7" type="local">Gated Linear Unit (GLU)</fr:link> splits the up-projection into two matrices whose outputs are multiplied elementwise, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\sigma (AW_{in1}) \odot  (AW_{in2})]]></fr:tex>, so one of them acts as a gate on the other. LLaMA, DeepSeek and most current models do this. A non-gated MLP has a single <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[W_{in}]]></fr:tex> and so <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2DF]]></fr:tex> params — and typically scales <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[D]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F]]></fr:tex> up to compensate, which is why the two families cost about the same at equal quality.

  The gate itself is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[O(BTF)]]></fr:tex> — elementwise, no contraction, and negligible against <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[BTDF]]></fr:tex>.
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors>
              <fr:author>
                <fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link>
              </fr:author>
            </fr:authors>
            <fr:date>
              <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
              <fr:month>8</fr:month>
              <fr:day>18</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/92HA/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>92HA</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/92HA/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The backward pass is two matmuls, not one">The backward pass is two matmuls, not one</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="anki">1787063264071</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>One matmul <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C = AB]]></fr:tex> with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A[N,P]]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B[P,M]]]></fr:tex> costs <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2NPM]]></fr:tex> FLOPs at inference but <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[6NPM]]></fr:tex> in training. Where do the other two thirds come from?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  The forward pass contracts over <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> and costs <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2NPM]]></fr:tex>. <fr:link href="/SGKK/" title="Backpropagation" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/SGKK/" display-uri="SGKK" type="local">Backpropagation</fr:link> then needs <html:em>two</html:em> matmuls of exactly that size, not one:

  <html:ul><html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\partial  L/\partial  B = A^{\top }(\partial  L/\partial  C)]]></fr:tex>, contracting over <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N]]></fr:tex> — the parameter gradient;</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\partial  L/\partial  A = (\partial  L/\partial  C)B^{\top }]]></fr:tex>, contracting over <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[M]]></fr:tex> — not a parameter gradient at all, but the signal handed to earlier layers.</html:li></html:ul>

  Each of the three contracts a different axis of the same <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N \times  P \times  M]]></fr:tex> box, so each costs <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2NPM]]></fr:tex> and the total is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[6NPM]]></fr:tex>, three times the forward pass. It is easy to remember only the first backward matmul and land on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[4NPM]]></fr:tex>; the activation gradient is the one that goes missing.

  <html:strong>Why the constant is worth memorising.</html:strong> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[PM]]></fr:tex> is the parameter count of that matrix and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N]]></fr:tex> is the token count, so <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[6NPM]]></fr:tex> is precisely the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[6 \cdot  \mathrm {params} \cdot  \mathrm {tokens}]]></fr:tex> rule of thumb — the whole training cost of a <fr:link href="/YB7M/" title="Transformer" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/YB7M/" display-uri="YB7M" type="local">Transformer</fr:link> is a sum of such terms.
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter>
